---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { content } from '../../i18n/ui';
import { altsFor } from '../../i18n/utils';
import { EMPRESA, empresaCompleta } from '../../lib/empresa';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const t = content[lang];
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  title={`${t.footer.privacy}, Ideasforge`}
  description="What information we collect on the Ideasforge site, what we use it for, who it is shared with and how we protect it."
  alternates={altsFor('privacy')}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-20 sm:px-8 sm:py-24">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-5xl">{t.footer.privacy}</h1>

    <div class="prose mt-10 max-w-none prose-headings:text-fg prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-accent prose-strong:text-fg">
      {/* The controller comes first because it is the first thing the reader
           needs: who holds their data and where to write. */}
      <h2>1. Who is responsible for your data</h2>
      {empresaCompleta ? (
        <>
          <p>
            The controller of the data you give us through this site is
            {' '}<strong>{EMPRESA.razonSocial}</strong>{EMPRESA.nif ? `, tax number ${EMPRESA.nif},` : ','}
            {' '}based at {EMPRESA.domicilio}, Spain.
          </p>
          <p>
            For anything to do with your data, including exercising the rights described below,
            write to
            {' '}<a href={`mailto:${EMPRESA.correo}`} class="link-inline">{EMPRESA.correo}</a>.
            We answer within one month at the latest.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you believe we have not handled your request properly, you can complain to the
            Spanish data protection authority, the <strong>Agencia Española de Protección de
            Datos</strong>, at
            {' '}<a href="https://www.aepd.es" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="link-inline">www.aepd.es</a>.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          <strong>Pending.</strong> This section is filled from <code>src/lib/empresa.ts</code>,
          and the checker refuses to publish the site while it is empty, so this page cannot ship
          with a hole where the controller’s identity belongs.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>2. What we collect, what for, and on what legal ground</h2>
      <p>
        This site has no user accounts and no private area. There are only three moments when we
        handle data about you, and in all three you know it is happening.
      </p>

      <h3>When you write to us through a form</h3>
      <p>
        The contact form collects your <strong>name, email, company, website and the message</strong>
        you write. The exploration form also collects the <strong>size of your company, the problem
        you want solved, where your data sits today, whether it can leave your infrastructure and
        the timeframe</strong> you have in mind.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use it for one thing: <strong>reading your case and answering you</strong>. We do not
        add you to any mailing list and we do not send you anything you did not ask for.
      </p>
      <p>
        The legal ground is <strong>Article 6(1)(b) of the GDPR</strong>, which covers steps taken
        before a contract at your own request. Giving us this data is voluntary, although without
        your email we have no way to reply.
      </p>

      <h3>When you simply visit the site</h3>
      <p>
        Our hosting provider keeps technical logs with your IP address and the browser you use, as
        any server does. They exist so the site works and so we can spot attacks, not to work out
        who you are. The ground is <strong>Article 6(1)(f)</strong>, our legitimate interest in
        keeping the site up and safe.
      </p>

      <h3>When you accept the measurement cookies</h3>
      <p>
        Only if you say yes. Google Analytics tells us which pages get read and which do not, as
        aggregate figures that do not identify you. The ground is your
        <strong> consent, Article 6(1)(a)</strong>, and you can withdraw it whenever you like from
        {' '}<button type="button" data-cookie-prefs class="link-inline">cookie preferences</button>
        {' '}in the footer. The detail is in the
        {' '}<a href="/en/cookies-policy" class="link-inline">cookies policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Who else sees your data</h2>
      <p>
        We do not sell your data and we do not hand it to anyone so they can sell you something.
        But three companies process it on our behalf so the site can work, and it is only fair
        that you know them by name.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, which hosts the site and serves the pages. It sees the IP
          addresses of people browsing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Web3Forms</strong>, which collects what you write in the forms and delivers it to
          us by email. It sees everything you send through them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong>, only if you accepted the measurement cookies. If you refuse,
          your browser never talks to Google at all.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>We will also hand data to the authorities where a law requires us to.</p>

      <h2>4. Data that leaves the European Union</h2>
      <p>
        The three companies above are American, so <strong>part of this processing happens outside
        the European Economic Area</strong>. We would rather say it plainly than bury it in a
        footnote.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those transfers rely on the mechanisms set out in Chapter V of the GDPR, which depending on
        the provider means their certification under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework or the
        standard contractual clauses approved by the European Commission. If you want to know which
        one applies to a given provider, write to us and we will tell you.
      </p>

      <h2>5. How long we keep it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>If your inquiry does not become a project</strong>, we keep it for one year from
          our last exchange, in case you pick the conversation back up. Then we delete it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If we end up working together</strong>, for as long as the relationship lasts and
          afterwards for as long as accounting and tax obligations require, which runs to six years.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Measurement data</strong> expires on its own after fourteen months.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Server logs</strong> are kept by the hosting provider for a short period, in the
          order of days.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Your rights</h2>
      <p>At any time, and without having to explain yourself, you can ask us to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Access</strong> your data and tell you what we hold.</li>
        <li><strong>Correct</strong> anything that is wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>Erase</strong> it, the so-called right to be forgotten.</li>
        <li><strong>Restrict</strong> what we do with it while a disagreement is settled.</li>
        <li><strong>Object</strong> to processing that rests on our legitimate interest.</li>
        <li><strong>Take it elsewhere</strong> in a format that is actually usable.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Withdraw your consent</strong> where that was the ground, without affecting what
          was done before.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write to the address in the first section. We charge nothing for it and we answer within
        one month at the latest. <strong>We take no automated decisions about you</strong> and we
        do not sort you into profiles.
      </p>

      <h2>7. How we protect it</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served encrypted end to end, the forms travel over a secure connection and only
        the person who has to answer them reads the messages. That said, and because we would
        rather warn than promise, <strong>no system connected to the Internet is invulnerable</strong>.
        If a breach ever happened that could affect you, we would tell you.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If we change how we handle data, we change this page first. When the change touches
        something you consented to, we will ask you again rather than assume.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
